{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>You shall not make with Me.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'You shall not make with Me' — you shall not make any form of silver or gold in partnership with Me for divinity; and it returned and said, 'You shall not make for yourselves' — at all, even not for divinity." "<b>And one may interpret</b> 'You shall not make with Me' — when you stand in prayer before Me, you shall not think about the silver and gold that is with you; for if you do so, I account it to you as though you made 'elohi of silver and elohi of gold.'" "<b>And Rashi, of blessed memory, wrote:</b> 'You shall not make with Me' — this comes to warn concerning the cherubim. That is, the cherubim that you are destined to make — 'with Me' — they shall not be of silver, for if you planned to make them of silver, they would be considered before Me as divinity. 'You shall not make for yourselves' — so that you not say, 'I am making cherubim in synagogues and study halls just as I make them in the eternal house.' And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in tractate Avodah Zarah: 'You shall not make with Me' — you shall not make what is with Me. And the verse comes to forbid the making of forms, both of those that serve in the upper domain, such as the holy creatures (חיות הקדש) and the ofanim and seraphim and the ministering angels, and those in the lower domain, such as the sun and the moon, stars and constellations. And since the verse forbade making the form of the ministering angels, which are in the likeness of a human being with the addition that they possess wings, I might understand that it would be permitted to make a human face alone; therefore they needed to interpret further: 'You shall not make with Me' — you shall not make Me — and that is the likeness of a human — so that there would be included in this prohibition all those who serve, all those in the upper domain and in the lower domain, and likewise a human face. And in Sanhedrin they interpreted it as a warning to the public not to appoint a judge for the sake of money but for the sake of wisdom; that is what is meant by 'You shall not make with Me elohi of silver and elohi of gold' — an elohah that comes because of silver and gold, for the name Elohim includes also judges." "<b>By way of the Midrash:</b> 'You shall not make with Me elohi of silver and elohi of gold' — you shall not act in the manner that people act toward the objects of their reverence: when good comes upon them they honor their reverence, as is said (Judges 16:23): ״נתן אלהינו בידינו את שמשון אויבינו״, and when calamity comes upon them they curse their reverence, as is said (Isaiah 8:21): ״והיה כי ירעב והתקצף וקלל במלכו ובאלהיו ופנה למעלה״. But you must give thanks both for the good and for the bad; thus said David, of blessed memory (Psalms 116:13–14): ״כוס ישועות אשא ובשם ה' אקרא״ — for the good; ״צרה ויגון אמצא ובשם ה' אקרא״ — for the bad. And so said Job (Job 1:21): ״ה' נתן וה' לקח יהי שם ה' מבורך״." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'You shall not make with Me' — I have already interpreted it in connection with the verse 'And He spoke,' that it is like 'face to face (על פני)'. And one may add in its explanation that it is as though it said 'iti' (אתי) with a tzere under the aleph, in the language of (Isaiah 6:5) ״ואראה את ה'״, for it is written (Exodus 33:20) ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״. Now Manasseh the wicked erred in this because he was not precise regarding the word 'et' (את), and therefore he seized Isaiah the prophet, saying to him: 'Moses your master said ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״, and you said ״ואראה את ה'״!' And this is what they interpreted in Yevamot at the end of the chapter HaChotzeitz: Manasseh said to Isaiah: 'Moses your master said ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״, and you said ״ואראה״.' He said: 'I know him, that he would not accept it; if I tell him, I will make him deliberate.' He said: 'I will hide in the cedar tree.' They brought the cedar tree and sawed it; when the saw reached his mouth, his soul departed — because he said (Isaiah 6) ״ובתוך עם טמא שפתים אנכי יושב״. And the word 'et' (את) and 'ot' (אות) are one, and that is what is written in the commandment of tekhelet (Numbers 15:39): ״וראיתם אותו״, which is from the language of 'et' (את). From this the Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: 'You shall not make with Me (אתי)' — 'You shall not make Me (אותי).' The discerning will find the explanation broad, for the word 'et' (את) is the 'ot' (sign), and understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {4 "Bracketed material [סגו\"ם] in the Hebrew appears to be a Masoretic annotation referring to the vowel pointing (segol) of the word אתי; rendered here according to its function in the grammatical argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:02.670899851Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}